      "id": 1,
      "text": "Danboro began as Clover Hill, a name chosen for its rich red clover‑suitable soil that farmers prized in the 1700s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/danboro_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The historic Danville Inn—later the Golden Lamb Hotel—was a key stagecoach stop between Philadelphia and Easton, hosting travelers and fox‑hunt events in the 1830s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        9,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/danboro_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Joseph Kaisinger, the inn’s landlord in 1833, advertised fox hunts in the Doylestown Democrat, drawing sportsmen to the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/danboro_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today Danboro remains a small rural village, its post office founded in 1870, surrounded by farmland and part of the greater Philadelphia metropolitan area.",
